  "decision": {
    "answer": "REVISIONS",
    "explanation": "The evidence from pmid-33035457 demonstrates that Tazemetostat is effective in patients with follicular lymphoma harboring EZH2 activating mutations, showing a higher objective response rate in the EZH2mut cohort compared to wild-type. However, the claim specifically asks about lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma (LPL), not follicular lymphoma. While LPL and follicular lymphoma are distinct B-cell neoplasms, the provided evidence does not support the efficacy of Tazemetostat for EZH2-mutated LPL. Therefore, the claim is not supported as written for the specified disease. A revision would be required to specify follicular lymphoma instead of lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma to align with the evidence.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "document_id": "pmid-33035457",
        "start": 0,
        "end": 409,
        "quote": "Activating mutations of EZH2, an epigenetic regulator, are present in approximately 20% of patients with follicular lymphoma. . . . Patients were categorised by EZH2 status: mutant (EZH2mut) or wild-type (EZH2WT). Patients received 800 mg of tazemetostat . . . The objective response rate was 69% (95% CI 53-82; 31 of 45 patients) in the EZH2mut cohort and 35% (23-49; 19 of 54 patients) in the EZH2WT cohort."
      }
    ],
    "unresolved": [
      "Evidence for Tazemetostat efficacy in EZH2-mutated lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma is missing."
